---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { siteData } from "../lib/data";
import { clipCount, failedModels, generatedModels, imageCount } from "../lib/models";
import { path } from "../lib/paths";

const models = generatedModels(siteData, "image");
const failed = failedModels(siteData, "image");
const imageTotal = models.reduce((n, m) => n + imageCount(m), 0);
const soundModels = generatedModels(siteData, "audio");
const soundFailed = failedModels(siteData, "audio");
const clipTotal = soundModels.reduce((n, m) => n + clipCount(m), 0);
const generatedAt = siteData.generated_at.slice(0, 10);
---

<Base
  title="About — Crungus Amongus"
  description="What the crungus is, how this archive was made, and what the consistency score does and doesn't mean."
>
  <article>
    <h1>About</h1>

    <h2>The word</h2>
    <p>
      In June 2022, Twitch streamer and comedian Guy Kelly typed
      <em>crungus</em> — a word he made up on the spot — into DALL·E&nbsp;mini
      (later renamed Craiyon) and got back nine pictures of the same snarling,
      hairy goat-demon. A word with no meaning had a consistent referent inside
      the model. The internet named it the first
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crungus">digital cryptid</a>.
      Craiyon v2 shipped in 2023 and the creature vanished from it.
    </p>

    <h2>The archive</h2>
    <p>
      This site asks the same question of every text-to-image model that will
      answer it: the models in Replicate's
      <a href="https://replicate.com/collections/text-to-image">text-to-image
      collection</a>, plus a hand-curated set of relics going back to
      <a href="https://replicate.com/kuprel/min-dalle">min-dalle</a> — the
      PyTorch port of DALL·E&nbsp;mini itself, still running on rented GPUs in
      2026. Each model was asked for ten images of “crungus” and ten of “a
      picture of a crungus”, with no other guidance: no style words, no seeds,
      no negative prompts. As of {generatedAt} the archive holds{" "}
      {imageTotal.toLocaleString("en-AU")} images from {models.length} models.
      {failed.length > 0 && ` ${failed.length} more were asked and produced nothing.`}
    </p>

    <h2>The sound</h2>
    <p>
      The same question, put to the models in Replicate's
      <a href="https://replicate.com/collections/ai-music-generation">music
      collection</a> plus <a href="https://replicate.com/suno-ai/bark">Bark</a>:
      ten clips of “crungus” and ten of “the sound of a crungus”, twenty
      seconds each where the model lets you ask, and otherwise whatever length
      it chooses. Models that need lyrics get the word itself as the lyric. No
      style words, no genre, no tempo.
      {clipTotal > 0
        ? ` The sound archive holds ${clipTotal} clips from ${soundModels.length} models; ${soundFailed.length} more were asked and produced nothing.`
        : " The sound archive is still being recorded."}
      Clips are loudness-normalised so the radio doesn't lurch between
      models, but otherwise untouched. Each model's clips get the same
      consistency score as the images, computed from
      <a href="https://github.com/LAION-AI/CLAP">CLAP</a> audio embeddings
      instead of CLIP, and it carries the same caveat in its audio form: a
      model with a strong house sound scores high whether or not it has a
      crungus.
    </p>

    <h2>The consistency score</h2>
    <p>
      What made the original crungus uncanny was not that the picture was
      frightening but that it was <em>repeatable</em>: ten samples, one
      creature. The score reported beside each model measures exactly that —
      the mean pairwise cosine similarity of CLIP embeddings across the
      model's ten outputs for a prompt. High means the word lands somewhere
      specific inside the model; low means each sample is a fresh guess. It
      says nothing about image quality, and a model that always paints the
      same beige void would also score high. Read it as: <em>does this model
      have a crungus?</em>
    </p>

    <h2>Refusals</h2>
    <p>
      Not every ask came back as a picture. Asked with each provider's default
      settings, some models handed back a safety block instead — the FLUX 2
      family between a quarter and nearly two thirds of the time, while most
      models never blocked at all. The block lands on the picture rather than
      the prompt, so the same model and the same word succeed on a different
      draw; nothing in the asking changed but the seed.
    </p>
    <p>
      Worse than the refusals were the ones that did not announce themselves.
      One model returned sixteen pure black frames and reported every one as a
      success, which is a silent hole in the archive rather than a visible one
      — and a black frame is an extreme outlier on the eigencrungi's most
      reproducible axis, which is brightness.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the archive is now generated with every safety control a model exposes
      turned as permissive as it goes: 37 of the image models have such a
      control, the rest have none and are asked exactly as before. That makes
      the corpus a record of what these models draw rather than of what their
      hosts will show. The refusal rates on the model pages are therefore a
      <em>dated measurement</em> and not a live statistic: they record what the
      defaults did up to the date shown, and they stop there. The pictures on
      the page are no longer the ones those numbers describe.
    </p>

    <h2>The atlas</h2>
    <p>
      The atlas embeds every image in the archive with CLIP and projects the
      embeddings to two dimensions with UMAP, so images that look alike sit
      together. Distances are only meaningful locally — clusters are real,
      the space between them is not.
    </p>

    <h2>The eigencrungi</h2>
    <p>
      Turk and Pentland's 1991 <em>eigenfaces</em> treated every face photograph
      as one point in pixel space and found, by principal component analysis,
      the directions that a stack of faces varies along most; any face is then
      a mean face plus a weighted sum of those components. The
      <a href={path("/eigen/")}>eigencrungi</a> page does the same to every
      image in the archive: centre-cropped, shrunk to 128 pixels, and
      decomposed with no alignment step, because there is nothing to align a
      crungus by. That is why the first components are light, framing and
      palette, and the creature only appears further down the list. Each
      slider moves one direction by up to three standard deviations of what
      the archive actually contains; the images beside it are the archive's
      real extremes.
    </p>
    <p>
      How much of a component is real? Split the archive into disjoint halves,
      decompose both and take the cosine between the two versions: nineteen of
      the twenty-four hold up above 0.7, and thirteen above 0.9. Only a
      component that clears 0.7 can be given a hand-name, by a human looking at
      the extremes; everything below it goes by number.
    </p>
    <p>
      That number used to be five. On the first archive of 1,594 images only
      five components reproduced, and the obvious reading was that the
      eigenvalue spectrum was to blame: past the fifth the eigenvalues sit so
      close together that the components looked free to rotate no matter how
      much data you threw at them. Ten times the images disproved it. The
      top-24 subspace overlap went from 0.82 to 0.97 and fourteen more axes
      became real, so the collapse was sample size all along. It is recorded
      here because the archive existed to test exactly that sort of claim, and
      this one was its own. The basis is left
      unrotated: varimax was tried, and on the CLIP basis it lifts the mean
      cosine from 0.61 to 0.71 while dropping the number of axes above 0.9 from
      two to none, because it rotates the reproducible components into the
      mixture. A few strong axes are worth more here than a dozen soft ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two things about those components are worth stating plainly. The first
      and most reproducible one is a light meter: its coefficient correlates
      with a picture's mean brightness at r&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.9967. It is also the
      component that most reflects which model was asked rather than what was
      asked for — an analysis of variance over the model labels explains 54% of
      its spread, against 16% averaged over all twenty-four. So the archive's
      single most reproducible axis is mostly a fact about house style and
      exposure, which is the unalignment result seen from another angle.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the headline slider is not a component at all. It is every image
      projected onto CLIP's <em>text</em> embedding of the word “crungus”,
      rendered back into pixel space by least squares. That direction is fixed
      by the prompt rather than estimated from the archive, so resampling
      cannot rotate it, and it means what a crungus-ness slider should mean. It
      is only weakly aligned with the unsupervised basis, and it is 46% model
      identity in its own right: the least crungus-like images in the archive
      come from ideogram, the most from recraft's SVG models.
    </p>

    <h2>Prior sightings</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crungus">Crungus</a> on
        Wikipedia
      </li>
      <li>
        Bruce Sterling's
        <a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/brucesterling/albums/72177720324124391/">
        Flickr archive</a> of the original Craiyon screenshots
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/image/explore">Artificial
        Analysis</a>, who compare current models on shared prompts (but keep
        no history)
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it cost</h2>
    <p>
      About <strong>A$1,500</strong>, almost all of it Replicate credit, for
      28,228 predictions of which 26,766 came back as a picture or a clip. The
      failures are mostly models that no longer run and refusals from the ones
      that do; both are recorded above rather than tidied away.
    </p>
    <p>
      The interesting part is not the total but how unevenly it was spent. The
      whole archive took 109 hours of billed GPU time, and two models from 2021
      and 2022 — <a href="https://replicate.com/nightmareai/disco-diffusion">Disco
      Diffusion</a> and <a href="https://replicate.com/pixray/text2image">pixray</a>
      — account for 23% of it while producing 1.5% of the images. Those two
      predate diffusion-as-we-know-it: each picture is an optimisation run,
      CLIP nudging an image toward a phrase over hundreds of steps. Disco
      Diffusion spends 262 seconds on one crungus. Nvidia's SANA Sprint,
      four years later, spends <em>0.2 seconds</em> — a thousand-fold speedup
      inside the span of a single archive, which is most of the story of this
      technology told as an invoice.
    </p>

    <h2>Colophon</h2>
    <p class="mono">
      images and clips generated via the Replicate API · scored with
      open-clip (ViT-B/32) and LAION CLAP · projected with UMAP · decomposed
      with numpy · encoded as
      AVIF and Opus
      (with an AAC fallback) · served from Tigris object storage · built with
      Astro · made by
      <a href="https://benswift.me">Ben Swift</a> at the ANU School of
      Cybernetics
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

